From 475019a9a1a541b35a24f2792907e042362f382e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luissalet <luismasc16@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 02:14:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/36] Pin the widened request surface and the recipe with
 tests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The compatibility guarantee is pinned as literal JSON rather than as a
spot check: a request naming none of the new fields must produce the
whole payload it produced before they existed. The other half is that
each new key stays off the wire until somebody sets it.

Also asserted, because each is a place the server would have accepted
the request and ignored the setting: eta, flow_shift and shifted_timestep
inside sample_params and not at the top level; hires target_width rather
than the CLI's target_w; extra_sample_args as a key=value string; and
that ad_model, ip_adapter, guidance_schedule, photo_maker and pulid
never appear anywhere in a payload.

The recipe tests cover the round trip, hash stability across key order,
hash instability across one more step, the diff naming both changed
paths, and that a missing model is reported rather than substituted.
SHA-256 is checked against the two published vectors so a bug in the
digest cannot bless its own output.

The migration test now proves what it claims. It creates the database at
v29, writes a project, a writing and a generated image into it, then
lets the real schema upgrade it and reads all three back — asserting
only the version number would have passed just as happily for a version
that dropped every store and recreated it empty.

The steps assertion moves from 150 to 100 to match the server's strict
clamp, and the visual-ref migration test follows the schema to v30.

